00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.790
And so when I first joined
the team here at Google Cloud,

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.550
we went out and we spent a lot
of time talking to customers.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.710
And what we kept
hearing over and over

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.650
is that advertisers know
that they have a lot of data.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.600
They understand that
the data is important.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.100
But it's really,
really challenging

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.890
to link these data
sets together and get

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.870
any sort of valuable insight.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.340
So here at Google, we decided
that we need to do something.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.030
And we started thinking about
how we could position ourselves

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:52.250
to best help our clients get
the most value from their data.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:55.020
And even personally, myself,
I came from the advertising

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.240
industry, online advertising.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.340
I worked at an
agency trading desk,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.230
and I heard these
same challenges

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.840
echoed over and over, I
would spend a lot of time

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.610
in Excel sheets doing
Vlookups, merging data sets,

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:13.020
just so we could deliver
those insights to our clients.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.010
So I think it's safe to say
that most companies understand

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.440
it's important to get
smarter about data.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.960
And actually, the
main goal is not just

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.820
to be smarter with
data, but to actually

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.920
put the customer at the center
of everything that you do.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.490
So with that in mind, I
wanted to fast forward a bit

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.519
and think about at the
end of the presentation.

00:02:30.519 --> 00:02:32.060
So if there are
three things that you

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.464
leave this presentation with--

00:02:34.464 --> 00:02:35.880
there are three
things that I want

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.790
to leave this presentation
with and maybe

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:41.490
think about as you hear from me
and some of our other partners.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.330
The first is, what are
the top three data silos

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:48.200
that you could combine to create
a better view of your customer?

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.720
How can you understand your
customer's journey better?

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.090
The second is, what
new customer insights

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.580
can you unlock by combining
your data together?

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.320
And then the third is how can
you connect audience Insights

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.410
to drive better media activation
and better performance?

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.030
So we know it's easy to
think about the problems,

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.190
but it's much more
difficult to get started.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.850
And so I hope we've inspired
you to take the first step

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.200
in your data journey.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.270
But even taking a
step back, how can we

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.540
get more valuable insights
from your customer's data?

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.910
We know that our
clients are generating

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.909
tons of data touchpoints.

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:27.450
I've heard one
statistic that there's

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.437
over 900 customer touchpoints
in the data journey.

00:03:30.437 --> 00:03:31.770
But how can you break this down?

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:36.400
How can you start making sense
from this huge volume of data?

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.400
And so one way that we
like to think about it

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.320
is with this digital
marketing insights formula.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.080
So if you think
about connections,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.760
or making connections across
data, then you get insights.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.380
And to break this down
even a little bit further,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.490
you can think of
an x and y-axis.

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:54.040
So if you have a
blank grid and you

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.259
draw one data point
on there, that

00:03:56.259 --> 00:03:57.550
doesn't actually tell you much.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.030
So you don't actually
know what's going on.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:00.560
You don't know what that means.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.740
And if you add two or three
additional data points,

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:04.540
that still doesn't
tell you much.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:06.250
What's really
valuable is the lines

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.050
that you draw in
between the data.

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:11.680
And when you start to add
those additional data sources,

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.660
the connections are the
really important part.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:17.075
It's the real-- the
true source of power.

00:04:17.075 --> 00:04:19.450
And so in order to see the
relationships between the data

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:21.620
points, you have to
create those connections.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.800
And when those
connections increase,

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.950
then you create a
three-dimensional picture

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.450
of your data, and you can start
to understand your customers

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:29.050
more.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:32.020
You can start to create
a richer and more robust

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.520
view of your
customer, which helps

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:36.820
you to customize and
personalize their journey

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:40.170
and create a really unique
customer experience.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:41.460
But here's the problem.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.747
Many of the tools that marketers
use exist in isolation.

00:04:45.747 --> 00:04:47.580
There are some really
great tools out there.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.500
But it's really challenging
to make connections

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.416
when you're using several
different tools that

00:04:51.416 --> 00:04:52.458
don't talk to each other.

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:54.166
It's as if all of
these tools were living

00:04:54.166 --> 00:04:56.460
in a separate desert island
with no boat, no bridge,

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.090
just hang out by themselves.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:01.510
The island itself might be
great, but if you're all alone,

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.692
it's not that fun.

00:05:03.692 --> 00:05:05.400
So we've heard from
some of our customers

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.070
that they use over 30
separate marketing tools

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.087
to deliver marketing-type
activities, online, offline,

00:05:11.087 --> 00:05:13.170
anything that you would
need to do with marketing.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:17.309
But in addition to marketing,
there's also customer data.

00:05:17.309 --> 00:05:19.100
And that data can live
in different silos--

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:22.380
your CRM data, your ERP
data, your billing data--

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.750
and all of this could be
enriching your marketing data

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.940
to create that fuller,
three-dimensional view

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:30.040
of your customer.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.180
So if you want to unlock
the power of your data,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.750
you need more than just a
new tool or one new thing.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.720
You need a whole customer data
platform, a holistic vision

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.600
that can help you to create the
connection between your data

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.030
points and pull out
the nuggets of data

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:49.827
to help you personalize the
experience for your customers.

00:05:49.827 --> 00:05:51.410
And integrating your
data doesn't just

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:53.000
drive a technical advantage.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.400
It's one thing to say you're
using the newest tools

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:57.077
and they're fast
and they're awesome.

00:05:57.077 --> 00:05:58.910
These tools are actually
critical to staying

00:05:58.910 --> 00:05:59.630
competitive.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.280
And the companies
that are doing that

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.880
tend to see huge benefits in
sales, profits, and margins.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:09.657
So why Google?

00:06:09.657 --> 00:06:11.740
And I love it when companies
come to this question

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.180
because it's really simple.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.310
So Google was built
as a data company.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:18.790
We've spent the past 18 years
developing advertising tools.

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:21.070
We have a huge
advertising business.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.170
And we did that because
we needed infrastructure

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.300
that was robust,
that was scalable,

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:26.770
that could manage a lot of data.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:28.478
And there wasn't
anything else out there.

00:06:28.478 --> 00:06:30.080
So we really built it ourselves.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.500
And we really aligned
that with our mission

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.630
of organizing the
world's information

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:38.000
and making it universally
accessible and useful.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.030
We also have a really
secure infrastructure

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:42.980
we take privacy and
security seriously.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:45.760
And our customers
put our trust in us.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:47.410
Our business depends on that.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:50.050
In fact, we've hired
over 500 engineers just

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.200
to focus on security alone.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.900
And at the end of the day,
security and infrastructure

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:56.820
is great.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.400
But for you, what really matters
is getting the insights faster.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.670
So we have pre-built
connectors that

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:07.110
can bring your data directly
into BigQuery, which reduces

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.000
your overall time to set up.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.880
And BigQuery alone can
run some of the queries

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:16.890
that you would normally run in
minutes or hours in seconds.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:19.470
Additionally, we want you to
focus on the results, not just

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:20.590
the technology.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.490
So we have a whole
no ops architecture,

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:25.320
which is unique among
Cloud providers.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.840
This means that you can
set up your data pipeline,

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.000
you can bring your data
in, and immediately

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.940
start to run a query
without the IT overhead

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:37.750
that you would need to use if
you were using other providers.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:39.790
So how do you get started?

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:41.470
I think the best way
to think about this

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:43.345
is to think about the
data sets that you want

00:07:43.345 --> 00:07:46.240
to bring in, and then think
about how you can get them

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:47.230
into BigQuery.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:50.650
One great starting point is
with the Google advertising data

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:51.680
sources.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.340
So we have a service called
the BigQuery Data Transfer

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:58.220
Service, which automatically
brings in data from YouTube,

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:02.710
DoubleClick, AdWords
automatically into BigQuery

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.489
in a scheduled manner
multiple times a day.

00:08:05.489 --> 00:08:07.030
And aside from this
transfer service,

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:08.970
we have native
connectors with GA 360

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:12.730
so you can bring in that really
rich, site-side analytics data,

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:16.690
and also Firebase, so you can
bring in your mobile app data.

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:18.730
Once your data is in
BigQuery, then you

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.760
can pretty much immediately
start querying it with BigQuery

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:23.380
and start to get insights.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.740
You can also leverage our
machine learning tools.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.530
So we have Cloud Data Lab, which
makes it easy to get set up

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:31.490
and start creating
your own models.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:34.510
You could use TensorFlow or
our machine learning engine,

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:37.510
or any of our other pre-built
machine learning APIs.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:40.470
You could also use some
visualization tools,

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:43.720
so partners like
Tableau, Looker,

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:46.402
or even connectors like Google
Sheets or other spreadsheets.

00:08:46.402 --> 00:08:47.860
BigQuery it makes
it easy to share,

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:50.650
and you choose who has
access to the data when.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:59.940
So we like to think about doing
marketing analytics on Google

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:03.540
Cloud through the lens
of the data lifecycle.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.640
So we have collect,
transform, and analyze.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:08.280
And we know that
most companies need

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.416
to do this before they can
get value out of their data.

00:09:11.416 --> 00:09:13.040
And we have tools
that help support you

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:15.027
every single step of the way.

00:09:15.027 --> 00:09:16.860
So in order to unlock
the power of your data

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.750
and create the meaningful
connections that

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:21.720
start to tell a
bigger story, it's

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.880
important to set
your base up right

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.500
and make sure you have a
strong data foundation.

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.090
So this is really just
beginning of the journey.

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:35.510
Google Cloud offers
a data platform

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:38.150
that's architecturally
different from most other SaaS

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:41.330
platforms in that it
allows you to integrate

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:44.540
huge data sets, many
different data sources,

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:47.420
in a scalable, open-ended way.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:49.220
We have open
architecture, which makes

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.920
it easy to bring in many
different data sources.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.680
You can merge those data sets,
you can use machine learning.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:58.160
The result is a platform
that is flexible and powerful

00:09:58.160 --> 00:09:59.960
that allows you
to really respond

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:01.850
to what your customers need.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:04.100
And we like to think of this
as the era of assistance,

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.170
meaning that
customers want to have

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:08.540
personalized,
unique experiences.

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:11.630
And when you use your
data to tell that story,

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:15.520
it makes it easier for
your company to do that.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:17.950
So I hope you've
enjoyed that overview.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:21.270
Next up I'm going to invite
Johnathan Romero to tell

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:24.450
a little bit about 7Stars and
their success story with Google

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:25.341
BigQuery.

00:10:25.341 --> 00:10:25.840
Thanks.

00:10:42.875 --> 00:10:44.250
JOHNATHAN ROMERO:
Thanks, Andrea.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:45.420
Really great stuff.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.030
So I'm Johnathan.

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:50.410
I work at the 7Stars
based in London.

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:53.040
And we are the UK's most
successful and largest

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:55.410
independent media agency.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.720
So we're a full service
agency doing everything

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:00.865
from TV and radio to the
whole digital spectrum.

00:11:00.865 --> 00:11:02.490
And we've been around
for several years

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:05.390
now and growing very quickly.

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:06.930
And as we've grown,
we've also seen

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:10.530
a big increase in the amount of
data that is available to us,

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:16.020
and the demand from clients for
sophisticated data projects.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:18.150
So we've really had
to look at our options

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:20.580
for managing that
data and ensuring

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:23.010
that we can effectively
activate it and make

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:27.127
it an asset for our
clients and internal teams.

00:11:27.127 --> 00:11:29.210
So today I'm going to start
just with a little bit

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:33.890
about the journey that led us
to Matillion and how we use it.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:36.820
When I started at the 7Stars,
one of the core requirements

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:40.285
was to overhaul the
reporting solution.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:48.550
The traditionally was based on
Excel with a lot of manual data

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:51.820
extraction from various
platforms, adding it into Excel

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:54.120
and using pivot tables
and things like that

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:57.130
to produce the final dashboard.

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:00.910
This was obviously a very manual
process, taking a lot of time,

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:03.700
and the outputs are
not particularly good.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:07.720
So we wanted to automate
as much as possible,

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:11.930
save a lot of time, and
improve the visuals in one go.

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:13.994
So we looked at various
options to do that.

00:12:13.994 --> 00:12:16.410
There are a lot of solutions
in the marketplace that offer

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:19.540
kind of one stop shop
digital marketing, data,

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:21.930
and reporting solutions.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.680
And so we looked around
at these services,

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:27.784
but nothing really gave us the
flexibility that we wanted.

00:12:27.784 --> 00:12:29.950
Each of our clients has
very different requirements,

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:32.050
and what works for one
client doesn't necessarily

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:33.950
work for another.

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:35.800
And it was during
this research phase

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:40.500
that we kept hearing
about BigQuery and GCP.

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:43.600
And we were very interested in
the DoubleClick data transfer

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:46.337
services that Google
were offering.

00:12:46.337 --> 00:12:47.920
And we were also
hearing more and more

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:50.890
about clients wanting
to upgrade to GA 360

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:52.570
with the BigQuery export.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:58.410
So BigQuery was becoming a
very viable solution for us.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:01.620
It was then quite easy
to make that decision

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:06.180
to move to BigQuery and put
all our data into BigQuery.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:08.870
It offered everything
we were looking for.

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:12.670
It's scalable,
maintenance-free, basically,

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:15.720
and we could pull
in the data from all

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.826
our Google Stack
advertising platforms.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:25.810
That left us with a hole
for various other platforms

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:27.780
like Facebook and Twitter.

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:31.240
Matillion is one solution
that will actually bring data

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:33.460
in from those
platforms when there

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:37.030
are several other third party
providers that all will hook up

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:39.370
to various other
advertising platforms

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:41.890
and pull the data
straight into BigQuery.

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:44.290
So it's really easy
and stress-free

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.530
to get your data into BigQuery,
and that was a big bonus for us

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:54.240
and one of the key and key
things that led us to BigQuery.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:57.130
So now you've got all
your data in BigQuery.

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:58.482
What do you do with it?

00:13:58.482 --> 00:13:59.440
How do you activate it?

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:01.720
How do you make sense of it all?

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:04.870
There's a lot of different data
sets, different schemas, not

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:08.860
particularly user friendly if
you're not a technical team.

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:13.270
So certainly for us, using
the DCM data transfer

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:17.200
files with billions and billions
of rows is not something

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:20.355
you can directly work with
a Tableau or a Data Studio.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.330
And BigQuery itself is great
for writing queries and getting

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:30.100
that immediate result.
But for reporting, what we

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:32.710
needed to have in place
was a replicable process,

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:34.120
these transformation
jobs that we

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:38.140
needed to build to aggregate
and clean the data for use

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:40.850
in reporting and other things.

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:43.960
And we didn't have a
big team of SQL experts.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:45.820
We didn't have a team
of data engineers

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:48.140
available to do that for us.

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:52.280
So we really needed a tool to
kind of bridge that gap for us.

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:55.690
And this is where
Matillion comes in.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:59.740
And we stumble upon Matillion
at a GCP summit in London.

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:02.440
And within five minutes
of a demo of Matillion,

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:05.020
we had decided that this was
a really great tool for us,

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:09.730
something that could help us
in the problem that we had.

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:15.250
So we signed up, we got it
going, and within a week or so,

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:18.610
we were very
comfortable with it.

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:20.530
We could do everything
we needed to do,

00:15:20.530 --> 00:15:26.140
so joining different data sets
together, aggregating, putting

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:28.960
various string calculations
and other kind of calculations

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:33.470
to really normalize the data
that we were working with.

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:36.130
So we really can do everything
we needed to do and take

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:40.210
that billion row of data table
down to maybe 30,000, 40,000,

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:42.081
which is much more manageable.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:46.410
And lastly, really,
the great thing

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:50.620
then is also be able to schedule
these transformation jobs.

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:54.820
So if you have consistent
data coming into BigQuery,

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:57.090
like within the data
transfer files, which

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:59.010
are coming in every
so often, you can then

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:02.490
schedule your Matillion
jobs to coincide with that,

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:08.130
and you have a consistently
updated data set for you.

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:10.050
So it's really great
to structure that data,

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:14.090
get it ready for reporting.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:16.940
The key, really, is getting
that lean and constant

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:20.300
consistent data set, where you
get much better performance

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:22.710
from things like Tableau
and Google Data Studio,

00:16:22.710 --> 00:16:27.390
where you've got your data
set up and ready to go.

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:31.010
So we've managed to build at
least 10 or so custom reports

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.290
since starting up
with Matillion,

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:38.090
saving hundreds of hours,
really, in work hours,

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:40.100
and letting those people
who would normally

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:42.860
be working with Excel to
actually go and do other things

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:47.620
and be much more effective.

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:49.220
And so I think,
finally, it's also

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:51.950
important to note that while
the reporting requirement was

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.060
the main driver
for us initially,

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:56.060
it's actually not necessarily
where you're going

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:58.860
to get all your value from.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:02.240
Since we've been using
Matillion for management

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:06.502
of the big raw data
sets we get from Google,

00:17:06.502 --> 00:17:08.960
you can then start to look at
things like advanced audience

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:13.609
segmentation, things you can't
do in DCM or GA interfaces.

00:17:13.609 --> 00:17:16.609
So you can get a much
more specific and detailed

00:17:16.609 --> 00:17:19.700
segmentation of your customers.

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:23.520
And specifically, if you are
using DBM for remarketing, then

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:27.200
the PLU process which takes
those audiences from BigQuery

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:31.940
and puts them into
DBM, that PLU process

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:33.880
can be automated
through Matillion,

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:36.470
which is a huge timesaver
because that's currently

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:40.580
a manual process if you're not
using something like Matillion.

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:42.710
And then working
with the GA 360 data

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:47.700
export, which is a very tricky
dataset to work with at times.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:50.750
So bringing Matillion
and putting that on top

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:54.350
is really beneficial.

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:57.110
We recently had a client
