00:26:47.740 --> 00:26:51.740
and all the joins must be
working for us to get this far.

00:26:51.740 --> 00:26:53.030
Paid just a little bit more.

00:26:53.030 --> 00:26:54.810
That's still raw data.

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:56.900
We might want to aggregate that.

00:26:56.900 --> 00:27:02.320
So I'm going to throw on a
quick aggregation, but also

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:03.370
a calculation.

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:11.830
What we're really
interested in here

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:15.700
is whether that marketing
data actually led

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:17.920
to the creation of an account.

00:27:30.510 --> 00:27:32.240
So we're going to add
a quick expression

00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:34.065
to say whether the
campaign was a success.

00:27:38.980 --> 00:27:42.700
That's going to be a
little bit more SQL.

00:27:42.700 --> 00:27:47.450
Now if we manage to find
the company in Salesforce,

00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:50.521
we can pick any field coming
from Salesforce, really,

00:27:50.521 --> 00:27:51.020
for this.

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:59.380
If that's no, then
that's not a success.

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:00.310
Otherwise it is.

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:02.770
Now you'll know your data
a lot better than I will.

00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:06.490
You'll also recognize that this
is a very simplified and very

00:28:06.490 --> 00:28:08.430
idealized scenario.

00:28:08.430 --> 00:28:11.150
But hopefully it gives you an
idea of what's going on here.

00:28:11.150 --> 00:28:14.350
I've now got zero or one
flag on every single record

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:16.870
based on whether that converted.

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:22.507
There's no errors, so let's
flow that into my aggregate.

00:28:22.507 --> 00:28:24.090
And this is where
you can really start

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:25.700
to get some powerful things.

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:33.600
We group by the campaign name.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:35.230
What we're primarily
interested in

00:28:35.230 --> 00:28:39.470
is how many people were
emailed, so we can just

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:40.740
count the records for that.

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:47.300
And we can sum those
zero, one flags

00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:49.750
to give us a meaningful
number of how many of those

00:28:49.750 --> 00:28:51.408
were respondents.

00:28:54.280 --> 00:28:56.970
And at this point
I can also go back

00:28:56.970 --> 00:29:00.660
see how many is going to make
it to that point in the flow

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:02.740
and have a look at it as well.

00:29:02.740 --> 00:29:04.440
This has been very
highly aggregated

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:07.000
down to just a
handful of campaigns.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.960
And you can see emailing a
couple of thousand people

00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:12.780
for each variation, and
we're getting response rates

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:14.594
of just under 1,000.

00:29:14.594 --> 00:29:17.010
If any of you guys are getting
that sort of response right

00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:18.990
from an email campaign,
do get in touch

00:29:18.990 --> 00:29:22.830
because I'd love
to hear about that.

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:24.360
One last thing.

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:26.430
I'm going to write that
out to a physical table

00:29:26.430 --> 00:29:28.650
so that we can share
that with other people

00:29:28.650 --> 00:29:30.100
without giving
them access to all

00:29:30.100 --> 00:29:34.450
that raw data with potentially
sensitive things in there.

00:29:34.450 --> 00:29:36.787
Give that a quick name.

00:29:36.787 --> 00:29:38.870
I don't know overwrite any
of the tables I've got.

00:29:38.870 --> 00:29:42.860
I'll just put a new one.

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:44.590
I'm going to run
this job in a second.

00:29:44.590 --> 00:29:48.180
But first, I'm going to make
it part of a repeatable flow

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:52.090
so that this can
be run repeatedly,

00:29:52.090 --> 00:29:55.170
and know that you're going to
get the same results each time.

00:29:55.170 --> 00:29:56.850
So I'm going to
drag my transform

00:29:56.850 --> 00:30:00.135
into the orchestration job
and then just put an and in

00:30:00.135 --> 00:30:04.050
to make sure that all my
prerequisite steps happen first

00:30:04.050 --> 00:30:05.700
before that
transformation happens.

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:16.283
So when my contacts are loaded
and my accounts are loaded

00:30:16.283 --> 00:30:22.090
and my Cloud storage
load completes,

00:30:22.090 --> 00:30:23.790
then I'm going to
run my transform.

00:30:23.790 --> 00:30:26.470
I'm just going to right click
and run this demo job now

00:30:26.470 --> 00:30:27.610
interactively.

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:29.320
That could, of
course, be scheduled.

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:31.870
It could be triggered on
the basis of that file

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:35.620
landing in the bucket
and triggered that way.

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:39.550
Also an API call
could kick that off,

00:30:39.550 --> 00:30:41.560
and that will now
run to completion.

00:30:52.100 --> 00:30:55.100
Just crossing an
extra pair of fingers.

00:30:55.100 --> 00:30:56.210
So far so good.

00:30:58.750 --> 00:30:59.250
Excellent.

00:30:59.250 --> 00:31:02.110
And now all my results
are in BigQuery as well as

00:31:02.110 --> 00:31:04.610
the source data, which
could now be removed.

00:31:04.610 --> 00:31:06.890
And we have a repeatable
float for doing that end

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:11.510
to end every time those
underlying data sources change.

00:31:11.510 --> 00:31:13.720
Hope you found that
useful and informative.

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:16.660
And I'd definitely invite
you all to hang around

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:17.770
for the next minute.

00:31:17.770 --> 00:31:20.189
I'm going to turn it
over to a Q&amp;A session.

00:31:20.189 --> 00:31:22.480
And I hope we've got plenty
of good questions coming in

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:23.721
for that.

00:31:23.721 --> 00:31:24.220
Thank you.

00:34:32.116 --> 00:34:34.199
ANDREA RUSSELL: Thanks for
that great demo, David.

00:34:34.199 --> 00:34:34.920
That was awesome.

00:34:34.920 --> 00:34:37.822
And I actually did
