00:36:55.710 --> 00:36:58.650
on their architectures,
solutions, tons of information

00:36:58.650 --> 00:37:00.310
about the industry.

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:02.100
And then the last question.

00:37:02.100 --> 00:37:06.150
So if I use the BigQuery data
transfer service or Matillion,

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:09.060
do I get any extra
information that's not already

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:10.440
in the public reporting API?

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:18.990
Yes.

00:37:18.990 --> 00:37:20.580
Actually, the answer is no.

00:37:20.580 --> 00:37:23.010
You actually don't get any
extra information that's

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:25.890
not included in the APIs.

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:30.300
What happens is we just pull
the information directly

00:37:30.300 --> 00:37:32.310
from the source where
it's coming from.

00:37:32.310 --> 00:37:35.850
So for example, the YouTube
API presents the data,

00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:37.140
I think, every day.

00:37:37.140 --> 00:37:40.300
The DFP API has a
different cadence.

00:37:40.300 --> 00:37:42.540
And we are just pulling
that data in and doing

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:44.100
that transformation for you.

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:46.650
The advantage of this data
is that it's a no op setup.

00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:50.070
So literally in five clicks,
you can bring your data

00:37:50.070 --> 00:37:53.370
in from those sources and you
can have it ready for analysis

00:37:53.370 --> 00:37:55.570
without having to
maintain that ETL.

00:37:55.570 --> 00:37:56.770
DAVID LANGTON: And that's
just kept up to date

00:37:56.770 --> 00:37:58.220
at all times, isn't
it, so you've never

00:37:58.220 --> 00:37:59.510
got to worry about that again.

00:37:59.510 --> 00:38:01.110
It's always just there
when you need it.

00:38:01.110 --> 00:38:02.360
ANDREA RUSSELL: Yeah, exactly.

00:38:02.360 --> 00:38:03.967
DAVID LANGTON: Yeah, perfect.

